WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.380 --> 00:00:06.540
In this video, we'll be introducing the 
concept of Evidence Based Medicine, or EBM.

2
00:00:07.380 --> 00:00:09.720
Evidence Based Medicine, or EBM,  

3
00:00:09.720 --> 00:00:15.300
is the integration of best research evidence 
with clinical expertise and patient values.

4
00:00:16.860 --> 00:00:21.420
EBM is frequently visualized using 
a Venn diagram that shows research  

5
00:00:21.420 --> 00:00:25.380
evidence, clinical expertise, and 
patient values joining together.

6
00:00:26.820 --> 00:00:33.000
The concept of Evidence Based Medicine comes out 
of work done in the 1970s at McMaster University  

7
00:00:33.000 --> 00:00:40.920
in Ontario, Canada. Dr. David Sackett, Dr. Brian 
Haynes, and Dr. Gordon Guyatt were among the first  

8
00:00:40.920 --> 00:00:45.720
to develop and write about this philosophy 
of medical practice and medical education.  

9
00:00:46.380 --> 00:00:52.620
The term EBM first appeared in print 
in 1991 in the ACP Journal Club.

10
00:00:54.960 --> 00:01:00.480
As the strength of EBM has become apparent 
other healthcare practitioners have developed  

11
00:01:00.480 --> 00:01:04.920
their own versions and the philosophy has 
spread beyond healthcare to other fields.

12
00:01:05.460 --> 00:01:10.200
Many people now prefer to use the more 
general term of Evidence Based Practice,  

13
00:01:10.200 --> 00:01:12.960
or EBP, to refer to the concept.

14
00:01:14.280 --> 00:01:21.060
EBM was the result of many factors, but some of 
the most influential were the increasing amount of  

15
00:01:21.060 --> 00:01:26.580
information available to physicians and the speed 
at which clinical information was being updated.  

16
00:01:27.240 --> 00:01:33.120
Today information overload is nothing new to us 
and most healthcare professionals accept that  

17
00:01:33.120 --> 00:01:38.100
they will not be able to read and understand 
all the new evidence before they need it.

18
00:01:39.540 --> 00:01:44.520
Evidence Based Medicine gives us a framework 
to find the evidence at the point of need.

19
00:01:46.200 --> 00:01:50.220
To practice EBM there are five steps 
that are generally agreed upon. 

20
00:01:50.220 --> 00:01:56.940
The first step is to assess your information need.
Step two is to convert your information need  

21
00:01:56.940 --> 00:02:02.340
into a focused answerable question.
Step three is to efficiently track  

22
00:02:02.340 --> 00:02:06.180
down and acquire the best evidence 
with which to answer the question. 

23
00:02:07.020 --> 00:02:11.880
Step 4 is to critically appraise the evidence 
for validity and clinical usefulness. 

24
00:02:12.660 --> 00:02:18.120
Step 5 is applying the results in clinical 
practice in combination with your clinical  

25
00:02:18.120 --> 00:02:22.440
expertise and the patient's values.
At any point in the cycle,  

26
00:02:22.440 --> 00:02:28.680
you may need to go back and redo a step.
And after step five, it is important to go  

27
00:02:28.680 --> 00:02:33.420
back and assess or evaluate the performance 
of the evidence in clinical application.

28
00:02:36.000 --> 00:02:39.720
There are several tools available 
to help you practice EBM.

29
00:02:40.980 --> 00:02:46.680
The PICO format is a tool to help you formulate 
an answerable clinical question. To find out  

30
00:02:46.680 --> 00:02:52.380
more about the PICO format, please visit the 
Asking Clinical Questions page of the EBM Guide.

31
00:02:53.760 --> 00:02:59.400
Another tool is the pyramid of evidence, which 
is a popular way of visualizing the amount  

32
00:02:59.400 --> 00:03:05.100
and hierarchy of medical literature. In the 
pyramid, the lowest levels represent evidence  

33
00:03:05.100 --> 00:03:10.680
that is plentiful but may not be effective or a 
valid methodology for clinical decision making. 

34
00:03:11.580 --> 00:03:16.440
In this version of the pyramid, we start 
with in vitro research, or bench science,  

35
00:03:16.440 --> 00:03:23.280
move up to animal research, and then ideas, 
editorials, and opinions. As we move further  

36
00:03:23.280 --> 00:03:28.740
up the pyramid, we move into clinical literature 
types with observational design, which is stronger  

37
00:03:28.740 --> 00:03:35.220
evidence. Examples of this are case reports, case 
series, case control studies, and cohort studies.  

38
00:03:35.940 --> 00:03:41.880
Next we have randomized controlled double-blind 
studies, and finally at the very top, we find  

39
00:03:41.880 --> 00:03:48.840
systematic reviews and meta-analyses, or our 
secondary literature. Systematic reviews and  

40
00:03:48.840 --> 00:03:54.900
meta-analyses are products of the EBM process.
This simple pyramid can be very helpful in  

41
00:03:54.900 --> 00:04:00.480
reminding us what to look for when answering our 
clinical questions; however, this is not the only  

42
00:04:00.480 --> 00:04:07.680
EBM pyramid. As with any simple representation of 
a complex concept, the pyramid continues to evolve  

43
00:04:07.680 --> 00:04:12.240
and several different versions have been proposed 
over the years since its first development.

44
00:04:13.980 --> 00:04:19.980
Another tool that was developed as a result of 
the EBM process are Clinical Queries. Clinical  

45
00:04:19.980 --> 00:04:25.260
Queries can be accessed from the PubMed home 
page and are a response to the clinician's  

46
00:04:25.260 --> 00:04:31.140
need to efficiently find evidence in a database 
that currently has over 35 million citations.

47
00:04:32.820 --> 00:04:38.220
One of the most important by-products of EBM has 
been the development of point-of-care clinical  

48
00:04:38.220 --> 00:04:44.580
tools like Dynamed, UpToDate, and Essential 
Evidence Plus, to name just a few. These tools  

49
00:04:44.580 --> 00:04:50.460
gather evidence, synthesize this evidence, and 
rate the evidence on its strength. They provide a  

50
00:04:50.460 --> 00:04:55.320
quick way to access evidence-based information 
to answer many of your clinical questions.

51
00:04:57.840 --> 00:05:00.780
You may still hear a few critics of Evidence Based  

52
00:05:00.780 --> 00:05:05.880
Medicine. Some have called it all statistics 
and algorithms or even "cookbook medicine."  

53
00:05:06.600 --> 00:05:12.480
However from its beginning, EBM has emphasized 
not just the research evidence but the  

54
00:05:12.480 --> 00:05:19.380
physician's clinical experience and the patient's 
values. EBM must involve all three components.  

55
00:05:19.380 --> 00:05:25.380
To practice EBM, the clinician finds the evidence 
and then evaluates the evidence in the context of  

56
00:05:25.380 --> 00:05:30.420
their knowledge and the needs of the individual 
patient before making a clinical decision.
